course: dictionary
lesson: 4090
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning is right. «Loud gigs need earplugs» is about live music, because loud and earplugs fit a concert.
explain: Ask yourself what would fit here, then try your meaning in the sentence. In «a 64-gig phone», the number and the word phone show that gig means a unit of computer memory, short for gigabyte.
explain: Gig is an informal word, so you will hear it in everyday talk more than in old books. When the context does not settle it, check a dictionary.
fact: A gig is most often a live music show or a comedy performance.
fact: «Play a gig» means to perform live, and gig can also be a verb, as in «They gig every weekend».
fact: A «gig economy» is work made of many small, one-off paid jobs.
fact: In computing, «gig» is short for gigabyte: «a 64-gig phone» has 64 gigabytes of memory.
fact: An old gig was a light, two-wheeled carriage pulled by one horse.
exercise: «Loud gigs need earplugs.» What is a gig here? | A short paid job | A unit of computer memory | A light carriage pulled by a horse | *A live music show
exercise: Sam says, «I bought a 64-gig phone.» What does gig mean here? | A pop concert | A paid job | A horse-drawn carriage | *A unit of computer memory, short for gigabyte
exercise: «Mia found a summer gig, working at the beach café.» What does gig mean here? | *A short, one-off paid job | A computer file | A band | A horse-drawn carriage
test: «The band will play a gig at the town hall on Friday.» What is a gig here? | A gigabyte of memory | A rule for the hall | *A live show | A horse and cart
test: «The band gigs every weekend.» What does gigs mean here? | *To play live music shows | To count bytes | To ride in a carriage | To sleep late
exam: Why do you check the words around gig before deciding its meaning? | Because gig is only ever a horse carriage | Because gig is always a computer word | Because context words never help | *Because gig has several meanings, and the context shows which one is meant
